-- title:
--   Degree bound for the reduction of a finite extension of 𝔽(x)
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb F$, $k$, $\kappa$ be fields in a common universe with $k$ and $\kappa$ both $\mathbb F$-algebras, and let $\kappa$ carry in addition the structure of an $\mathbb F[X]$-algebra compatible with its $\mathbb F$-algebra structure (a scalar tower $\mathbb F \to \mathbb F[X] \to \kappa$); write $x = \mathrm{algebraMap}\ \mathbb F[X]\ \kappa\ X$ for the image of the variable and $L_0 = \mathbb F(x)$ for the intermediate field `IntermediateField.adjoin 𝔽 {x}` of $\kappa/\mathbb F$. Assume $\kappa$ is finite-dimensional over $L_0$. Let $\Omega$ be a field which is both a $k$-algebra and an $\mathbb F$-algebra, with scalar tower $\mathbb F \to k \to \Omega$, and let $\rho \colon \kappa \to \Omega$ be an $\mathbb F$-algebra homomorphism; put $t = \rho(x)$ and assume $t$ is transcendental over $k$. Let $E = k(\rho(\kappa))$ be the intermediate field of $\Omega/k$ generated by the range of $\rho$, and inside $E$ let $K''$ be the intermediate field of $E/k$ generated by the element $t$ (viewed as an element of $E$). Then $[E : K''] \le [\kappa : L_0]$ as finranks, i.e. $\dim_{K''} E \le \dim_{L_0}\kappa$.
--
--   This is the degree inequality in the Deuring–Igusa reduction lemma: the field generated over $k$ by the reduction $\rho(\kappa)$ of a finite extension $\kappa$ of the rational function field $\mathbb F(x)$ has degree over $k(t)$, $t = \rho(x)$, at most the generic degree $[\kappa:\mathbb F(x)]$. It is used in the construction of two-chart integral models of curves, where the reverse inequality is a hypothesis and the two together express that the reduction attains the generic degree.
-- source:
